\title{Checking is super-linear for some large shapes that use associated types}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-15}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{Measured against the same program without associated types. None of these sizes is likely in ordinary code.}
\table{
\tr{\th{Shape}\th{Growth}\th{Largest measurement}\th{Control}}
\tr{\td{An associated type reached through a chain of N supertraits}\td{quadratic time and memory}\td{N = 12800: 7.2 s, 5.1 GB}\td{0.15 s}}
\tr{\td{N bounds on one parameter, each associated type named once}\td{cubic time}\td{N = 6400: 25.7 s}\td{N = 3200: 0.10 s}}
\tr{\td{A record literal with N fields whose type is an associated type}\td{quadratic time and memory}\td{N = 12800: 4.5 s, 1.3 GB}\td{0.12 s}}
\tr{\td{A forwarding impl nested N deep, at monomorphization}\td{about cubic}\td{N = 62: 0.21 s}\td{0.02 s}}
}
\p{The last shape cannot grow further, because nesting deeper than 64 hits the depth limit of trait selection. A long chain of one binary operator is also slower than linear, with or without associated types: 12800 terms of an addition take about one second. Found by the fuzzing of associated types recorded in [ses-TKVW](ses-TKVW).}
